@thockin I was thinking more about this problem, it's caused because the APIserver is not setting the IPFamily field but, in reality kube-proxy can infer always the IPFamily, right?

I think that kube-proxy should not be aware of the Service IPfamily field and infer it directly from the service IP, what do you think?

This is how it's done with the endpoints

https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/f3df7a2fdb16dd7b00a9d357e337ed48a6f70d45/pkg/proxy/metaproxier/meta_proxier.go#L203-L229

We just need to add a new function:

```go
// servicesIPFamily that returns IPFamily of services or error if
// failed to identify the IP family.
func servicesIPFamily(service *v1.Service) (*v1.IPFamily, error) {
```

This way  we smooth the upgrade process, avoiding these panics and removing the apiserver dependency from kube-proxy

